      }
    },
    {
      "id": "engine-matches-formal-semantics",
      "kind": "invariant",
      "statement": "The Rust engine and the Lean formal semantics give identical traces on every golden and on every input of a recorded seeded run of the input generator, whose coverage of the language's constructs is stated.",
      "rationale": "Supported by tests/core/formal-semantics.test.js (WG-4685): the Lean executable reproduces every golden trace, and the native Rust engine and the Lean executable give identical traces on every golden input and on 150 inputs of seed 4685 of tools/gen-engine-inputs.js, whose construct coverage the test checks and tools/differential.js reports. It is evidence on the inputs run, not a proof that the engine refines the semantics.",
      "subjects": [
        "simulation-engine",
        "formal-semantics"
      ],
      "assumptions": [
        "tests-exercise-shipped-modules",
        "recorded-results-reflect-current-code"
      ],
      "evaluate": {
        "method": "external-evidence"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "resource-queues-respect-bounds",
      "kind": "invariant",
      "statement": "At every settled step of every format-2 run, the units a request class holds never exceed its limit, the units held never exceed capacity (except units held across a mid-run capacity cut, which are never preempted), waiting requests never exceed the class and resource bounds, bounds are enforced class by class in declaration order and then for the resource, and the request removed is the one the overflow rule names (refuse and drop: the newest; shed-oldest: the oldest of the class, or for the resource bound the oldest overall under fifo and the oldest of the lowest-priority class under priority), with the matching refused event and reason.",
      "rationale": "Planned (WG-4664; specified in docs/simulation.md, Resources). To be proved in Lean as an invariant of the step and checked differentially on generated inputs. Negative twin: a fixture whose arrival rate exceeds capacity with a class bound of 3 under each overflow rule must remove exactly the named request; engine mutants that check bounds before granting, refuse the oldest instead of the newest, or shed across classes for a class bound fail it. Not evaluated until recorded evidence exists.",
      "subjects": [
        "resource-model",
        "simulation-engine",
        "formal-semantics"
      ],
      "assumptions": [
        "tests-exercise-shipped-modules",
        "recorded-results-reflect-current-code"
      ],
      "evaluate": {
        "method": "external-evidence"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "resource-utilisation-at-most-one",
      "kind": "invariant",
      "statement": "Service demand conserves work: for every resource and run, the integral of units held over the window never exceeds the integral of the capacity in effect (while no mid-run capacity cut leaves units held above it), a completed use request held its units for exactly ceil(draw times demand factor times scale), and the sum of completed service times equals the busy integral when the window covers the run.",
      "rationale": "Planned (WG-4664, WG-4715; specified in docs/simulation.md, Resources and Measured outputs). Negative twin: engine mutants that grant without checking free units, release before service-done, or apply the demand factor after rounding report a busy integral above the capacity integral or different from the sum of service times on the saturated fixture, and the test must fail on them. Not evaluated until recorded evidence exists.",
      "subjects": [
        "resource-model",
        "performance-metrics",
        "simulation-engine"
      ],
      "assumptions": [
        "tests-exercise-shipped-modules",
        "recorded-results-reflect-current-code"
      ],
      "evaluate": {
        "method": "external-evidence"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "format-1-unchanged-under-format-2",
      "kind": "invariant",
      "statement": "Format 1 is removed and every conformance golden is version 2: for each golden, its trace minus the version-2 fields equals its version-1 trace at the base commit of tests/conformance/golden-changes.json, or the difference is listed, with its reason, in that manifest; simulationInput derives a version-2 input for every specification.",
      "rationale": "WG-5681 removed format 1 and lift_agrees with it. tests/core/conformance.test.js checks the manifest against git (goldenChanges in tools/conformance.js), so a golden that changes without an entry fails; tools/differential.js --lift prints the same comparison. Negative twin: editing a golden trace without a manifest entry fails the test. Not evaluated until recorded evidence exists.",
      "subjects": [
        "simulation-contract",
        "simulation-engine",
        "formal-semantics"
      ],
      "assumptions": [
        "tests-exercise-shipped-modules",
        "recorded-results-reflect-current-code"
      ],
      "evaluate": {
        "method": "external-evidence"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "engine-matches-formal-semantics-v2",
      "kind": "invariant",
      "statement": "The Rust engine and the Lean semantics give identical version-2 traces and metrics on every version-2 golden and on every input of a recorded seeded run of the version-2 input generator, whose coverage of instances, routes, timers, resources and their overflow rules, links, workloads, distributions and resource faults is stated.",
      "rationale": "Planned (WG-4664, WG-4715), as engine-matches-formal-semantics is for format 1. Evidence on the inputs run, not a proof that the engine refines the semantics. Negative twin: the differential tool must report a mismatch, with the first differing step and field, for an engine mutant that grants under fifo by class priority, and for one that rounds a transfer time down; a generator run whose coverage omits a construct is reported, not counted as agreement on it. Not evaluated until recorded evidence exists.",
      "subjects": [
        "simulation-engine",
        "formal-semantics",
        "simulation-contract"
      ],
      "assumptions": [
        "tests-exercise-shipped-modules",
        "recorded-results-reflect-current-code"
      ],
      "evaluate": {
        "method": "external-evidence"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "metrics-reproduce-mm1-utilisation",
      "kind": "measured",
      "statement": "The M/M/1 fixture (Poisson arrivals at 800/s, exponential service of mean 1 ms, one server, unit us, warm-up 20 s, horizon 220 s) reproduces utilisation 0.8 within 2% by an approx metric claim at 95% confidence over 59 seeded runs.",
      "rationale": "Fixture specified in docs/simulation.md, Fixtures (queueing theory); planned (WG-4715). Its parameters are tagged assumed or to be measured; a holding result is model-holds, never supported. Negative twin: the same runs with set-capacity 2 (utilisation 0.4) must fail the claim. Not evaluated until recorded evidence exists.",
      "subjects": [
        "performance-metrics",
        "workload-model",
        "resource-model",
        "metric-claims"
      ],
      "assumptions": [
        "tests-exercise-shipped-modules",
        "recorded-results-reflect-current-code",
        "seeded-streams-independent"
      ],
      "evaluate": {
        "method": "external-evidence"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "response-time-counts-failures-as-unbounded",
      "kind": "invariant",
      "statement": "In every response-time distribution, a measured request that ended refused, timeout or error (a lost start included, by the request lifecycle), or is censored, counts as +infinity, so only ok requests are finite and a mean over a class with any such request is undefined; a claim restricted to outcomes ok reports how many requests it left out.",
      "rationale": "Planned (WG-4715; specified in docs/simulation.md, Measured outputs). Negative twin: in the pool fixture's collapse-classes twin most moves are refused quickly; a metrics implementation that took their short end - start as samples would make the twin pass, and the test must fail on such a mutant. Not evaluated until recorded evidence exists.",
      "subjects": [
        "performance-metrics",
        "metric-claims"
      ],
      "assumptions": [
        "tests-exercise-shipped-modules",
        "recorded-results-reflect-current-code"
      ],
      "evaluate": {
        "method": "external-evidence"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "metrics-reproduce-mm1-mean-wait",
      "kind": "measured",
      "statement": "The M/M/1 fixture reproduces the mean waiting time in queue rho / (mu - lambda) = 4 ms within 5%, by an approx metric claim on the median of the per-run means at 95% confidence over 59 seeded runs (the order statistics 22 and 38), with the measured gap between the median of per-run means and the analytic mean stated beside the tolerance.",
      "rationale": "Fixture specified in docs/simulation.md, Fixtures (queueing theory), with base, control and twins; planned (WG-4715). Parameters are tagged assumed or to be measured; a holding result is model-holds, never supported. Negative twin: the fault replace-demand with a fixed 1 ms service (M/D/1, mean wait 2 ms) must fail the claim. Not evaluated until recorded evidence exists.",
      "subjects": [
        "performance-metrics",
        "workload-model",
        "resource-model",
        "metric-claims"
      ],
      "assumptions": [
        "tests-exercise-shipped-modules",
        "recorded-results-reflect-current-code",
        "seeded-streams-independent"
      ],
      "evaluate": {
        "method": "external-evidence"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "metrics-reproduce-mm2-erlang-c",
      "kind": "measured",
      "statement": "The M/M/2 fixture (lambda = 1600/s, mean service 1 ms, two servers) reproduces the Erlang C mean waiting time C(2, 1.6) / (2 mu - lambda) of about 1.78 ms within 5% at 95% confidence over 59 seeded runs.",
      "rationale": "Fixture specified in docs/simulation.md, Fixtures (queueing theory); planned (WG-4715). Fixture results test the engine and method, not any system. Negative twin: the variant mm2-split (two capacity-1 resources fed by a random route, so two M/M/1 queues at 800/s each with mean wait 4 ms; a round-robin split would be E2/M/1 at about 2.84 ms) must fail the claim, which shows the multi-server grant is not two queues. Not evaluated until recorded evidence exists.",
      "subjects": [
        "performance-metrics",
        "resource-model",
        "metric-claims"
      ],
      "assumptions": [
        "tests-exercise-shipped-modules",
        "recorded-results-reflect-current-code",
        "seeded-streams-independent"
      ],
      "evaluate": {
        "method": "external-evidence"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ladder-reacts-without-flapping",
      "kind": "measured",
      "statement": "On the ladder fixture, a behavior reading a meter of refused page reads rises to rung 1 within 10 s of the flood start (time-to-first), makes at most 4 rung transitions during the flood, and returns within 30 s after it (time-to-recover), each at 95% confidence over 59 runs.",
      "rationale": "Fixture specified in docs/simulation.md, Fixtures (load-shedding ladder); planned (WG-4715). Its parameters are tagged assumed or to be measured; a holding result is model-holds, never supported. Negative twin: equal rise and lift thresholds and no hold (a faultOnly transition enabled) flap, with more than 20 transitions, and the transition-count claim must fail. Not evaluated until recorded evidence exists.",
      "subjects": [
        "performance-metrics",
        "metric-claims",
        "workload-model"
      ],
      "assumptions": [
        "tests-exercise-shipped-modules",
        "recorded-results-reflect-current-code",
        "seeded-streams-independent"
      ],
      "evaluate": {
        "method": "external-evidence"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "retry-budget-bounds-amplification",
      "kind": "measured",
      "statement": "On the retry fixture (2,000 client behaviors with a 500 ms timeout, a database slowdown of 5 times from 20 s to 30 s), the busiest second of queries reaching the pool during the slowdown, relative to the mean per second over 10 to 20 s (the ratio form of metric-bound), is at most 1.5 when clients retry within a 10% budget, at 95% confidence over 59 runs.",
      "rationale": "Fixture specified in docs/simulation.md, Fixtures (retry amplification), with base, control and twins; planned (WG-4715). Parameters are tagged assumed or to be measured; a holding result is model-holds, never supported. Control: no slowdown. Negative twin: the unbudgeted retry transition (faultOnly, enabled) amplifies the ratio beyond 3 and the claim must fail; the same transition in the control must not. Not evaluated until recorded evidence exists.",
      "subjects": [
        "workload-model",
        "performance-metrics",
        "metric-claims"
      ],
      "assumptions": [
        "tests-exercise-shipped-modules",
        "recorded-results-reflect-current-code",
        "seeded-streams-independent"
      ],
      "evaluate": {
        "method": "external-evidence"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "reconnect-stagger-bounds-handshake-wait",
      "kind": "measured",
      "statement": "On the reconnect fixture (4,000 client behaviors on 4 hosts, host 0 crashed at 30 s with a connection-reset notice and restarted after 2 s), clients that reconnect after a uniform 0 to 5 s stagger keep the p90 wait for the host CPU (TLS handshake demand) at most 200 ms in the window 30 to 40 s, at 95% confidence over 59 runs; about 1,000 handshakes per run leave 100 samples beyond the p90.",
      "rationale": "Fixture specified in docs/simulation.md, Fixtures (reconnect storm), with base, control and twins; planned (WG-4715). Parameters are tagged assumed or to be measured; a holding result is model-holds, never supported. Control: no crash. Negative twin: an immediate reconnect (stagger 0) must fail the claim, and the same transition enabled in the control must not. Not evaluated until recorded evidence exists.",
      "subjects": [
        "resource-model",
        "workload-model",
        "metric-claims"
      ],
      "assumptions": [
        "tests-exercise-shipped-modules",
        "recorded-results-reflect-current-code",
        "seeded-streams-independent"
      ],
      "evaluate": {
        "method": "external-evidence"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "address-limiter-acts",
      "kind": "measured",
      "statement": "On the address-limiter fixture (50 attacking addresses at 100/s each, a limiter keyed on address admitting 20/s), at least 90% of attack requests are refused, at 95% confidence over 59 runs.",
      "rationale": "Fixture specified in docs/simulation.md, Fixtures (address limiter), with base, control and twins; planned (WG-4715). Negative twin: a variant of the base whose limiter limit variable starts at 1,000/s per address refuses no attack request and the claim must fail. Not evaluated until recorded evidence exists.",
      "subjects": [
        "workload-model",
        "performance-metrics",
        "metric-claims"
      ],
      "assumptions": [
        "tests-exercise-shipped-modules",
        "recorded-results-reflect-current-code",
        "seeded-streams-independent"
      ],
      "evaluate": {
        "method": "external-evidence"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "address-limiter-false-positives-bounded",
      "kind": "measured",
      "statement": "On the address-limiter fixture, legitimate users grouped onto addresses (up to 50 behind one shared address) see a false-positive refusal ratio of at most 0.001, at 95% confidence over 59 runs.",
      "rationale": "Fixture specified in docs/simulation.md, Fixtures (address limiter), with base, control and twins; planned (WG-4715). Negative twin: a variant of the base whose limiter limit variable starts at 2/s per address refuses users behind shared addresses and the claim must fail. Not evaluated until recorded evidence exists.",
      "subjects": [
        "workload-model",
        "performance-metrics",
        "metric-claims"
      ],
      "assumptions": [
