      }
    },
    {
      "id": "bulkhead-keeps-static-served-under-db-slowdown",
      "kind": "measured",
      "statement": "On the hold-across fixture (16 API threads held while calling the database, a database slowdown of 10 times from 20 s to 40 s, static requests at 200/s), with database calls limited to 8 threads and a maximum wait, the p99 of static requests in the slowdown window is at most 50 ms, at 95% confidence over 59 runs.",
      "rationale": "Fixture specified in docs/simulation.md, Fixtures (holding across a downstream wait), with base, control and twins; planned (WG-4715). Parameters are tagged assumed or to be measured; a holding result is model-holds, never supported. Control: no slowdown. Negative twin: collapse-classes on the thread pool lets database calls hold all 16 threads, static requests starve, and the claim must fail; in the control it must not. Not evaluated until recorded evidence exists.",
      "subjects": [
        "resource-model",
        "performance-metrics",
        "metric-claims"
      ],
      "assumptions": [
        "tests-exercise-shipped-modules",
        "recorded-results-reflect-current-code",
        "seeded-streams-independent"
      ],
      "evaluate": {
        "method": "external-evidence"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "crash-instance-loses-state",
      "kind": "invariant",
      "statement": "A crash-instance fault stops exactly the named instance at its time: its incarnation is incremented, its timers removed, its waiting requests removed with their timeouts, its in-service requests ended with their service-done entries and its units released, its restart drawn, its notify event broadcast; every targeted entry created for the old incarnation, a reply to a call it made before the crash included, is dropped as stale; a routed entry decided for it is ignored by that behavior only, and a broadcast skips it; instance aggregates exclude it while it is down; round-robin, random, any and least-busy routes skip it; a restart edge restores only its own incarnation; a crash of a missing or already down instance does nothing; and on restart the instance is in its initial state with its initial variables.",
      "rationale": "Planned (WG-4715, defined once here for WG-4704's crash-restart; specified in docs/simulation.md, Faults in format 2). Negative twin: client C calls server S, C crashes and restarts, S replies: the reply must be dropped as stale; two overlapping crashes of one instance must not restart it at the first restart edge; engine mutants that deliver the reply to the new incarnation, restart early, or route to a down instance because it holds nothing fail it. Not evaluated until recorded evidence exists.",
      "subjects": [
        "resource-model",
        "simulation-engine",
        "formal-semantics"
      ],
      "assumptions": [
        "tests-exercise-shipped-modules",
        "recorded-results-reflect-current-code"
      ],
      "evaluate": {
        "method": "external-evidence"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "distribution-lowering-bit-exact",
      "kind": "invariant",
      "statement": "The lowering (formal/Exegra/Lower/Dist.lean) lowers distributions with integer arithmetic only: the exponential and normal quantile tables are produced by the integer algorithms of docs/simulation.md and equal the correctly rounded values at the 1,045 grid points, their canonical text hashing to SHA-256 a23cd2a9566c106701375d5cf75ef982b8db6f3e52c199c47e6239649c840950; exp_fp, ln_fp and the exponential, log-normal and empirical constructions reproduce every reference output bit for bit; and a lowered value of 2^53 base units or more, a sigma above 8 or a p99 above 10^6 times the median is refused.",
      "rationale": "Built (WG-4715) in src/core/distributions.js and moved to Lean (formal/Exegra/Lower/Dist.lean, Families.lean, Pinned.lean, WG-5683); the reference values of exp_fp, ln_fp, the log-normal sigma, Qexp and Qnorm are also #guard checks there. Negative twin: an implementation that evaluates the log-normal quantile with Math.exp or AS 241 in doubles, rounds sigma instead of flooring it, or computes the normal CDF at 2^-96 instead of 2^-192, must fail the table hash or a reference output; a log-normal of median 1 ms and sigma 9 must be refused. The correctly rounded values were checked once against an 80-digit computation when the algorithms were specified; the test pins them through the hash.",
      "subjects": [
        "workload-model",
        "simulation-contract"
      ],
      "assumptions": [
        "tests-exercise-shipped-modules",
        "recorded-results-reflect-current-code"
      ],
      "evaluate": {
        "method": "external-evidence"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "poisson-arrivals-by-inversion",
      "kind": "invariant",
      "statement": "Poisson arrivals, constant or ramped, take exactly one arrival draw per instant, computed by inverting the integrated rate with exact integer square roots and rounding to the nearest base unit; no valid input is refused or fails for rejected candidates, and a draw whose instant falls at or after its phase end or the horizon creates nothing.",
      "rationale": "Planned (WG-4715; specified in docs/simulation.md, Workloads). Negative twin: a ramp from 0 to 10^7/s over 1000 s in unit ns, which would need about 1.4 x 10^5 rejections under thinning, must run with one draw per instant; an implementation that rounds interarrivals down instead of to the nearest unit must fail the fixture that measures the arrival rate of a constant phase at a mean interarrival of 100 base units. Not evaluated until recorded evidence exists.",
      "subjects": [
        "workload-model",
        "formal-semantics"
      ],
      "assumptions": [
        "tests-exercise-shipped-modules",
        "recorded-results-reflect-current-code"
      ],
      "evaluate": {
        "method": "external-evidence"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "overlapping-faults-follow-active-set",
      "kind": "invariant",
      "statement": "With windowed faults that overlap on one resource, the value in effect at every fault-edge is the declared function of the active set: the last active set-capacity, set-class-limit or replace-demand in the order of the input's faults list, the product of the active scale-demand factors reduced to lowest terms, and collapsed while any collapse-classes is active; demand and scale are evaluated at the grant against the faults active then; when a collapse ends, waiting requests return to their own classes in (since, seq) order; and under a collapse each request keeps its class for maxWait and metrics.",
      "rationale": "Planned (WG-4715; specified in docs/simulation.md, Faults in format 2). Negative twin: two overlapping set-capacity faults whose order in the input is swapped must give different capacities during the overlap; an engine mutant that applies the most recently started fault instead of the last in input order, or that leaves requests in the collapsed queue after the collapse ends, fails it. Not evaluated until recorded evidence exists.",
      "subjects": [
        "resource-model",
        "formal-semantics"
      ],
      "assumptions": [
        "tests-exercise-shipped-modules",
        "recorded-results-reflect-current-code"
      ],
      "evaluate": {
        "method": "external-evidence"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "broadcasts-evaluated-at-delivery",
      "kind": "invariant",
      "statement": "A broadcast reaches the instances that are up when it is delivered (keyed instances created since its creation and instances restarted since included) and skips down ones without an ignored entry; targeted entries keep the instance and incarnation fixed when they were created; routed entries are decided, with their counters, draws and incarnation, when first taken as head, and a delayed copy keeps the decision; and the next arrival instant of a workload is created exactly once, even when a fault delays or drops the first entry of the current instant.",
      "rationale": "Planned (WG-4664, WG-4715; specified in docs/simulation.md, Instances and Workloads). Negative twin: a broadcast at time 10 to a behavior whose instance crashed at 5 and restarted at 8 must reach the restarted instance while a reply created at 7 for its old incarnation is dropped; an arrival created at 6 and routed least-busy, delivered at 9, must see the load at 9 and must not be stale; a delayed first arrival must not create the next instant twice. Engine mutants that route at creation or recreate the instant fail it. Not evaluated until recorded evidence exists.",
      "subjects": [
        "simulation-engine",
        "workload-model",
        "formal-semantics"
      ],
      "assumptions": [
        "tests-exercise-shipped-modules",
        "recorded-results-reflect-current-code"
      ],
      "evaluate": {
        "method": "external-evidence"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "request-lifecycle-defines-outcomes",
      "kind": "invariant",
      "statement": "Every request of a class follows the request lifecycle of docs/simulation.md: it starts when its start entry is first taken as head; it ends once, at the first delivered end event with its key (a deliver step that does not fail, whether or not a transition fires), as error at once when its start entry is dropped, crashed, stale or handled by no transition, or as timeout on a closed-client timeout; it is censored otherwise; only ok requests are finite samples; and every sample count, bucket power and derived metric is computed from these outcomes.",
      "rationale": "Planned (WG-4715; specified in docs/simulation.md, Request lifecycle). Negative twin: a fixture whose start event is dropped by a drop-event fault, whose start event reaches a behavior with no transition for it, and whose start is routed to a crashed instance must record three error ends at the step that lost them, and a p99 claim over it must count them as +infinity; a metrics implementation that leaves lost starts out of the samples, or ends a request on a dropped end event, fails it. Not evaluated until recorded evidence exists.",
      "subjects": [
        "performance-metrics",
        "metric-claims",
        "formal-semantics"
      ],
      "assumptions": [
        "tests-exercise-shipped-modules",
        "recorded-results-reflect-current-code"
      ],
      "evaluate": {
        "method": "external-evidence"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "conformance-never-hides-mismatch",
      "kind": "invariant",
      "statement": "The conformance runner reports agreement only when no golden trace differed.",
      "subjects": [
        "simulation-contract",
        "engine-goldens",
        "engine-conformance-agrees",
        "engine-conformance-diverges"
      ],
      "assumptions": [
        "machines-abstract-the-code"
      ],
      "evaluate": {
        "method": "state-invariant",
        "scenarios": [
          "engine-conformance-agrees",
          "engine-conformance-diverges"
        ],
        "expr": "conformance-flow.reportedAgree => conformance-flow.mismatches == 0",
        "negatives": [
          {
            "scenario": "engine-conformance-diverges",
            "faults": [
              "conformance-ignores-mismatch"
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "faultModel": "local-workstation"
    },
    {
      "id": "app-and-cli-run-lean-engine-only",
      "kind": "invariant",
      "statement": "The application and the command line run scenarios on the Lean engine only: no module of src/app, src/ui or cli/ loads the Rust engine or runs a scenario any other way.",
      "rationale": "Not evaluated: no evidence attached. Read from the code when this was corrected: src/core/engine.js is the only module that loads an engine, and it calls engine.run through the Lean call boundary (src/core/lean.js); the Rust engine is loaded only by tools/rust-engine.js and tests. A test that lists the imports of src/app, src/ui and cli/ and fails on any reference to the Rust engine would make this claim evidence-backed.",
      "subjects": [
        "simulation-engine",
        "session",
        "agent-cli"
      ],
      "assumptions": [
        "tests-exercise-shipped-modules"
      ],
      "evaluate": {
        "method": "external-evidence"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "implementation-properties-declarative",
      "kind": "invariant",
      "statement": "The implementation-level properties (level, instances.storedIn, delivery, elapsed, an emit effect's to, transaction, otherwise, ignores) never change simulation: the engine input derived by simulationInput does not contain them and a specification that uses them parses and validates like any other. The one exception is the declared types of an implementation-level behavior (variable repr, payload field types), which reach the engine input only as the behavior's lowered types, never as repr.",
      "rationale": "docs/semantics.md states that these properties are declarative and names the Lean definition that stays normative for each machine construct; since WG-4902 the declared types make arithmetic checked (claim expression-semantics-shared). The lowering (formal/Exegra/Lower/Input.lean, Exegra.Lower.engineInput) builds behaviors and transitions field by field and strips storedIn and an emit's to. Supported by the core tests over the fixture machines.",
      "subjects": [
        "simulation-contract",
        "validator"
      ],
      "assumptions": [
        "tests-exercise-shipped-modules",
        "recorded-results-reflect-current-code"
      ],
      "evaluate": {
        "method": "external-evidence"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "runtime-fault-kinds-simulated",
      "kind": "invariant",
      "statement": "The fault kinds duplicate-event, lose-event and retry-event derive a version-2 engine input; the engine delivers an entry a duplicate-event matches and queues a copy of it by later (default 0), recorded duplicated; drops an entry a lose-event matches, recorded lost; and delays an entry a retry-event matches by the sum of the retry backoff waits after its attempts, recorded retried.",
      "rationale": "WG-4903. Normative: dropMatches, delayMatches and duplicateAfter in formal/Exegra/Format2.lean; the shell computes a retry's delay (src/core/fault-model.js, retryDelay) and the Rust oracle (engine/exegra-engine/src/v2.rs) mirrors the semantics. Supported by the core test over the runtime-faults fixture.",
      "subjects": [
        "simulation-engine",
        "simulation-contract"
      ],
      "assumptions": [
        "tests-exercise-shipped-modules",
        "recorded-results-reflect-current-code"
      ],
      "evaluate": {
        "method": "external-evidence"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "pooled-quotas-executable",
      "kind": "invariant",
      "statement": "quotaPool is an entity kind and a scenario lists the pools it runs; a pool runs as one machine per pooled limit and modelled member, whose transitions admit usage on headroom (within the fair share when throttled), throttle a member over its fair share at the end of the stated consecutive windows unless its exemption applies from that window, release after the stated windows under, and record an exemption from the window it is set in or the next; simulation and the Lean explorer run these machines, so a claim over them is decided by exploration and a wrong rule (eligibility effective only from the next window, or a longer over-share window) yields a counterexample; and the pool rules are reported at their positions.",
      "rationale": "Evidence is the core tests of WG-5278 (tests/core/pooled-quotas.test.js) on the Harbourline BR-12 fixture: the machines and their derivation, a throttle and its release in a simulated run, exploration that holds for the burst and three-day claims and returns a counterexample for each seeded wrong rule, retroactive and default exemptions, and each validation rule broken once. Supported, not verified, because Exegra does not check its own derivation. The decision is dec-pooled-quotas.",
      "subjects": [
        "simulation-contract",
        "validator",
        "metamodel"
      ],
      "assumptions": [
        "tests-exercise-shipped-modules",
        "recorded-results-reflect-current-code"
      ],
      "evaluate": {
        "method": "external-evidence"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "lowering-in-lean-reproduces-recorded-inputs",
      "kind": "invariant",
      "statement": "The engine input of a scenario is derived in Lean (Exegra.Lower.engineInput, formal/Exegra/Lower/Input.lean) and called through src/core/engine.js; it equals the input the deleted JavaScript lowering (src/core/sim-input.js, pools.js, distributions.js) derived for every scenario variant recorded in tests/fixtures/lowering/inputs.json and for every golden input, the refusal message included when the scenario is refused, and the distribution tables Lean builds equal, byte for byte as JSON text, the tables recorded in tests/fixtures/lowering/tables.json for every distribution family of the queueing fixtures and the reference families.",
      "rationale": "WG-5683 (P3) moves the lowering to Lean so that the engine runs on the lowering of a model and a scenario. The fixtures were recorded from the JavaScript lowering before its deletion; tests/fixtures/lowering/timed.jsonc exercises every branch of the timed lowering (resources, links, workloads, quota pools, resource and retry faults, distributions) and its refusals. Negative twin: a lowering that keeps an emit's to, rounds a rate instead of reducing it, or takes the first of two entities with one id instead of the last, fails an entry of the fixture.",
      "subjects": [
        "simulation-contract",
        "workload-model"
      ],
      "assumptions": [
        "tests-exercise-shipped-modules",
        "recorded-results-reflect-current-code"
      ],
      "evaluate": {
        "method": "external-evidence"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "events-belong-to-interfaces",
      "kind": "structural",
      "statement": "Every event of the specification belongs to the interface of an element or a port, every cross-element state read goes through an observable that a declared interface exports, and every declared interface lists its members' boundary events.",
      "rationale": "WG-5697 criterion 1. A read of state that a declared interface does not export is a validation error (behavior.reads-unexported).",
      "subjects": [
        "refinement-checker",
        "transactions",
        "session",
        "simulation-engine",
        "persistence"
      ],
      "evaluate": {
        "method": "interfaces-closed"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "scenario-environments-declared",
      "kind": "temporal",
      "statement": "In every scenario, each delivered event that an omitted element would receive is covered by an environment assumption of the scenario's behavioural cut: commit-reevaluates-claims states the simulation engine as its environment for claim-scenarios-run.",
      "rationale": "WG-5697 criterion 5. Without the engine's environment assumption, commit-reevaluates-claims reports claim-scenarios-run (the negative).",
      "subjects": [
        "simulation-engine",
        "claim-evaluator",
        "commit-reevaluates-claims"
      ],
      "assumptions": [
        "engine-answers-run-scenario"
      ],
      "evaluate": {
        "method": "environment-assumed",
        "scenarios": [
          "visual-edit-flow",
          "source-edit-flow",
          "rejected-edit",
          "edit-with-disk-conflict",
          "write-with-stale-base-hash",
          "app-start-and-open",
          "production-read-only-edit",
          "conflict-resolved-by-reload",
          "conflict-resolved-by-overwrite",
          "external-change-adopted",
          "own-write-notice-ignored",
          "undo-restores-and-saves",
          "layout-move-saved",
          "commit-reevaluates-claims",
          "export-after-save",
          "import-bundle",
          "import-unparseable-bundle",
          "generate-from-panel",
          "real-write-recorded",
          "simulation-panel-run",
          "simulation-reload-cancels-run",
          "preferences-remembered",
          "camera-remembered",
          "cli-apply",
          "cli-apply-partial-write",
          "cli-verify-with-open-session",
          "cli-trace-written",
          "cli-trace-refused",
          "prove-accepted",
          "prove-lean-rejects",
          "markdown-import",
          "markdown-import-coverage-fails",
          "engine-conformance-agrees",
          "engine-conformance-diverges",
          "live-session",
          "two-session-edit-race"
        ],
        "negatives": [
          {
            "scenario": "commit-reevaluates-claims",
